(Descirption)
Implement pow(x, n), which calculates x raised to the power n (i.e., xn).

--- Solution ---
class Solution {
    public double myPow(double x, int n) {
        long N = n;

        if (N < 0) {
            N = -N;
        }

        double result = 1.0;
        double current = x;

        while (N > 0) {
            if (N % 2 == 1) {
                result *= current;
            }

            current *= current;
            N /= 2;
        }

        if (n < 0) {
            return 1.0 / result;
        }

        return result;
    }
}

--- Approach ---
To calculate the power, if the given n value is negative it needs to convert the given n value to the positive.
However because the difference between maximum int value & minimum one, it needs the bigger bowl for n, that was long data type from int.
N value was absolutely same with n, but it was covered by long data type, it could convert the power value into positive without any error.

To find the power, it uses while loop statement until that the power value becomes 0 when it calculates at the exact power numbers.
Because (x^n)^n = x^2n in the while loop, it powers to the powered value with dividing N by 2.
When there is leftover, it multifly to the result, then all of the times of leftover works like a milestones to recalculate the result.
And at the last time, the N value always become 1 then 0, so that it could calculate the final result and return it in safe.
